 and Property Damage coverage. Continue No vehicle? You probably don't need car insurance. Need car insurance to drive a car that doesn't belong to you? You need a specialty policy called Non-Owners Insurance. (Unfortunately, we don't sell it.) Let’s say your car was damaged by a storm or by an accident you caused. Do you want your insurance to help pay to repair or replace it? Yes No You chose Comprehensive/Collision coverage. Good call! It’ll help pay to repair or replace your vehicle in a bunch of unpleasant circumstances, like theft, storm damage, or an at-fault accident. (You’re in good company, too. Most people add this to their policy.) Continue Okay. Are you currently making payments on your vehicle? Yes No You’re likely required to purchase Comprehensive/Collision coverage. It’s mandatory for financed cars. ( Comprehensive/Collision coverage pays to repair or replace your vehicle in a bunch of unpleasant circumstances, like theft, storm damage, or an at-fault accident. Most drivers purchase it.) Continue Got it. We won’t add Comprehensive/Collision to your list for now. Consider it for the future, though! It’s a useful coverage that helps to repair or replace your vehicle in a bunch of unpleasant circumstances, like theft, storm damage, or an at-fault accident. Continue Do you want your car insurance to cover your medical bills, no matter who caused the accident? Yes No I'm not sure Good choice! Uninsured/Underinsured Motorists Bodily Injury coverage help you pay your medical expenses if you’re in an accident caused by someone who either isn’t insured or doesn’t have enough insurance to cover your bills. (1 in 8 US drivers is uninsured!) Medical Payments and Personal Injury Protection are coverages that help cover your medical expenses after an accident—even if you're at fault. Continue Okay, we won’t add Medical Payments (or similar Personal Injury Protection) or Uninsured/Underinsured Motorist Bodily Injury to your list for now. Keep in mind that if you don’t get these coverages, you’ll need to pay for all your post-accident medical bills some other way—either out-of-pocket"
